From e3f03d9a8f4729a75d4d31b41b682efbae1459e7 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Cursor Agent <cursoragent@cursor.com>
Date: Mon, 12 Oct 2026 12:23:24 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 2/9] feat(plugin-sdk): sign plugin artifacts with Ed25519

The artifact signer used HMAC-SHA256, so the "public" verification key
was the signing key itself: whoever could verify a plugin could also
forge one. verify() trusted the digest carried by the artifact whenever
the content was absent, never looked at the algorithm field, and the
signature did not cover the manifest, so the permissions a plugin asks
for could be changed after signing.

Replace it with Ed25519 signatures over a domain-separated payload of
the algorithm, the artifact content digest and a canonical manifest
digest. verify() now takes the artifact content and the manifest,
recomputes both digests itself, picks the verification key from an
explicit set of trusted public keys by key id, and rejects unsigned
artifacts, unknown algorithms, untrusted keys, digest mismatches and
bad signatures. The signature is a detached JSON document
(PluginSignature) that travels with the artifact.

Plugin authors get key helpers (generate_private_key, public_key_pem,
load_trusted_keys, ...) and "python -m forgemesh.plugin.signtool" with
keygen, sign and verify subcommands; keygen writes the private key with
mode 0600 and never overwrites an existing file.

Add the "cryptography" package (>=42) as a dependency of
forgemesh-plugin-sdk and to requirements-dev.txt: the standard library
has no asymmetric signature primitives, and cryptography is the
maintained binding to audited implementations that pip installs from
wheels on the supported platforms, including the python:3.12-slim
image the control plane is built on.

PluginSigner.sign() and verify() change their signatures and the
PluginArtifact model of the signing module is removed; only tests
called them.
